<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tokugawa Yoshinobu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">resigned <a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tokugawa Yoshinobu was the fifteenth and last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a>. Facing mounting pressure from anti-shogunate domains and the challenges posed by Western powers, he resigned his powers to the emperor in 1867. Accounts of the Tokugawa Shogunate treat his resignation as the end of shogunal rule, which was followed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshinobu.jpg" alt="Tokugawa Yoshinobu" width="259" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frederick Sutton · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1867_Osaka_Yoshinobu_Tokugawa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Tokugawa Yoshinobu was born in Edo on 28 October 1837, the seventh son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-nariaki">Tokugawa Nariaki</a>, daimyō of Mito of the Tokugawa branch family of Mito. He was raised in Mito from the age of seven months and educated in the literary and martial arts and in government. At his father's urging he was adopted by the Hitotsubashi-Tokugawa family to improve his chances of the succession, became family head in 1847 and took the name Yoshinobu.</p><p>When the thirteenth shōgun, Iesada, died in 1858, Yoshinobu was nominated as a possible successor, but the opposing faction led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naosuke">Ii Naosuke</a> prevailed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-iemochi">Tokugawa Yoshitomi</a> became the fourteenth shōgun, Iemochi. Yoshinobu was placed under house arrest in the Ansei Purge, but after Ii's assassination in 1860 he was reinstated and in 1862 became the shōgun's guardian. In 1864, commanding the defence of the imperial palace, he defeated Chōshū forces who tried to seize the Hamaguri Gate in the Kinmon Incident.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Iemochi died without issue in August 1866. Shogunal officials nominated Yoshinobu, who became head of the Tokugawa main family in October but refused the title of shōgun until January 1867. He was the only Tokugawa shōgun to spend his entire tenure outside Edo, and never set foot in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-castle">Edo Castle</a> as shōgun. He began a government overhaul, with a French military mission.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">Samurai</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a>, Chōshū and Tosa allied against him, fearing a renewed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>. On 9 November 1867 Yoshinobu tendered his resignation to the Emperor, stepping down formally ten days later, and withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka">Osaka</a>. Satsuma and Chōshū, however, secured an imperial edict later shown to be a forgery, and at a court meeting he was stripped of all titles and land.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>When Tokugawa troops marched towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> to deliver a protest they were attacked, which began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-toba-fushimi">Battle of Toba–Fushimi</a>, the first clash of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a>. Yoshinobu left his army when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> and Chōshū forces raised the imperial banner, and escaped to Edo. On 11 April 1868 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-castle">Edo Castle</a> was handed over to the imperial army and the city was spared all-out war. He then moved to Shizuoka and lived in quiet retirement.</p><p>On 3 June 1902 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji">Emperor Meiji</a> allowed him to found his own branch house with the rank of prince. He sat in the House of Peers until 1910 and died in November 1913, and he is buried in Yanaka Cemetery, Tokyo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tokugawa Yoshinobu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under mounting pressure, the last shogun, <b>Tokugawa Yoshinobu</b>, returned his governing authority to the young Emperor Meiji in November 1867, and the restoration of imperial rule was proclaimed on 3 January 1868.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>On 8 November 1867 <b>Tokugawa Yoshinobu</b> returned power to the Imperial Court, and on 3 January 1868 domain troops seized the Imperial Palace as the Emperor declared his authority restored.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Nariaki's son became <b>Tokugawa Yoshinobu</b>, the last shogun.</blockquote><a href="/state/mito-domain">Mito Domain</a></li><li><blockquote>The last lord, Ikeda Yoshinori, a brother of the last shogun <b>Tokugawa Yoshinobu</b>, nevertheless sided with the imperial forces in 1868.</blockquote><a href="/state/tottori-domain">Tottori Domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Koishikawa, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Koishikawa-ku, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.71,139.75&amp;year=1867" aria-label="Koishikawa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8078 874 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Tokugawa Yoshinobu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8528 1144h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8528 1144h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.71,139.75&amp;year=1867">What was here in 1867? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
